meta {
  name: 9c4. Verify a forged session cookie does not authenticate (→ 401)
  type: http
  seq: 25
  tags: [
    requires-nifi
  ]
}

get {
  url: {{apisixApiUrl}}/v1/datasets/{{sagaDataSetId}}/{{sagaSlug}}
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Cookie: session=Zm9yZ2VkLXNlc3Npb24tc3RhdGUtbm90LXNpZ25lZA
}

tests {
  test("A forged/garbage session cookie does not grant access (401)", function() {
    // With bearer_only=false the openid-connect plugin processes session cookies, so the session
    // secret is a real credential. The secret is never a known committed constant (seed-routes.sh
    // generates a per-run random one; prod injects), so an attacker cannot mint a VALID session.
    // A forged/garbage cookie must fail to decrypt → treated as anonymous → OPA denies → 401.
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(401);
  });
}

docs {
  **Saga step 9c4** — Session-cookie forgery guard.

  Because `unauth_action: pass` requires `bearer_only: false`, APISIX's openid-connect plugin still
  processes session cookies — the `session.secret` is a real authentication credential, not an inert
  schema value. The secret must never be a known committed constant (seed-routes.sh uses a per-run
  random value; production injects a strong one). This test sends a **forged session cookie** on an
  unauthenticated request to a protected dataset and asserts the gateway denies it (**401**): a
  garbage cookie fails to decrypt and the caller is treated as anonymous. It guards against a
  regression that re-introduces a known/weak session secret or otherwise trusts client session state.

  **Precondition:** runs while the dataset is protected (before 9f).
}
